This is considered the most-searched event

00:11:45.462 --> 00:11:46.970
in the history of the internet.

00:11:46.970 --> 00:11:50.440
And the search is Janet
Jackson exposing her breast.

00:11:50.440 --> 00:11:53.670
It's not Justin Timberlake
ripping off a piece of clothing.

00:11:53.670 --> 00:11:54.830
That is not the search.

00:11:54.830 --> 00:11:57.210
The search is Janet Jackson.

00:11:57.210 --> 00:12:00.740
Okay, my last takeaway, and
this is the one that like,

00:12:00.740 --> 00:12:02.134
I don't really understand

00:12:02.134 --> 00:12:04.906
and I want to have a
better understanding of

00:12:04.906 --> 00:12:08.230
and I'm actually left a little
bit confused which is that

00:12:08.230 --> 00:12:11.990
humans love this stuff, humans love it.

00:12:11.990 --> 00:12:14.031
Our brain, for some reason, is predisposed

00:12:14.031 --> 00:12:18.930
to love and fixate and just
devour this sort of content.

00:12:18.930 --> 00:12:22.799
It's human psychology at its
most basic and predictable form

00:12:22.799 --> 00:12:25.040
and I don't really understand why.

00:12:25.040 --> 00:12:26.170
Let's remember the setting.

00:12:26.170 --> 00:12:28.480
This is 2004, early 2004.

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:29.580
It's an election year.

00:12:29.580 --> 00:12:31.340
George Bush is up for election.

00:12:31.340 --> 00:12:34.967
A few months earlier George
Bush and his administration

00:12:34.967 --> 00:12:39.130
had led us into a war in Iraq which,

00:12:39.130 --> 00:12:41.190
as I've covered in previous videos,

00:12:41.190 --> 00:12:43.290
was largely a war of choice.

00:12:43.290 --> 00:12:45.250
And now our taxpayer money is being used

00:12:45.250 --> 00:12:47.620
to wage a war halfway around the world

00:12:47.620 --> 00:12:51.740
looking for fake WMDs and yet this,

00:12:51.740 --> 00:12:53.810
this half a second of an exposed breast

00:12:53.810 --> 00:12:56.000
was the epicenter of our attention.

00:12:56.000 --> 00:12:57.330
This is what mattered.

00:12:57.330 --> 00:13:01.630
This is what warranted discussion
and congressional hearings

00:13:01.630 --> 00:13:03.950
and tons of conversation.

00:13:03.950 --> 00:13:07.000
This woman and her body and her behavior.

00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:08.682
- [Reporter] What part of the Super Bowl

00:13:08.682 --> 00:13:11.561
did you like the best,
the halftime or the game?

00:13:11.561 --> 00:13:13.340
(onlookers laughing)

00:13:13.340 --> 00:13:14.820
- I'm not trying to be
the woke police here.

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:16.810
I'm not trying to convince you of anything

00:13:16.810 --> 00:13:19.510
other than what I am seeing
and what I am finding

00:13:19.510 --> 00:13:21.708
as I look at this with clear perspective

00:13:21.708 --> 00:13:25.880
and with some understanding of
how language and media works

00:13:25.880 --> 00:13:27.740
now that I've lived a little bit of life.

00:13:27.740 --> 00:13:29.360
If I'm missing something, let me know.

00:13:29.360 --> 00:13:30.730
But let me be clear about something.

00:13:30.730 --> 00:13:32.640
This wasn't an isolated incident.

00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:34.920
This sort of biased coverage and scrutiny

00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:38.451
wasn't new in 2004 and
it certainly didn't stop.

00:13:38.451 --> 00:13:41.070
I remember when I first
started working at Vox.

00:13:41.070 --> 00:13:43.440
We would have these
meetings and discussions

00:13:43.440 --> 00:13:44.900
about the YouTube comments.

00:13:44.900 --> 00:13:46.740
And I was publishing stuff on YouTube.

00:13:46.740 --> 00:13:49.010
I was showing my face
more and we were growing

00:13:49.010 --> 00:13:51.330
and it was exciting and I
loved to read the comments

00:13:51.330 --> 00:13:53.170
and be like, whoa, look
at all this excitement

00:13:53.170 --> 00:13:54.450
around the work we're doing.

00:13:54.450 --> 00:13:57.150
I was sort of naive and confused at first

00:13:57.150 --> 00:13:58.630
because I would put a video up

00:13:58.630 --> 00:14:00.230
and get all these cool comments.

00:14:00.230 --> 00:14:03.030
Wow, this guy's traveling
the world, it's super cool.

00:14:03.030 --> 00:14:04.770
And then I would read the comments

00:14:04.770 --> 00:14:06.520
any time we would put up a video

00:14:06.520 --> 00:14:09.670
where a woman, a female was the host.

00:14:09.670 --> 00:14:11.720
The comments were always
so much more active

00:14:11.720 --> 00:14:13.970
and not with discussion about the video

00:14:13.970 --> 00:14:15.800
but about the host herself.

00:14:15.800 --> 00:14:18.470
Her voice, her hair, her clothing.

00:14:18.470 --> 00:14:21.340
I'm married to somebody who
makes videos for the internet.

00:14:21.340 --> 00:14:23.830
I see every comment that comes in.

00:14:23.830 --> 00:14:27.180
I see what you say to her
versus what you say to me.

00:14:27.180 --> 00:14:30.030
It's as predictable as
my children's behavior.

00:14:30.030 --> 00:14:32.766
It is like a law of
nature that the comments

00:14:32.766 --> 00:14:37.766
on her videos will focus so
much on her touching her neck

00:14:37.920 --> 00:14:41.170
or the voice she's using
or her interrupting me.

00:14:41.170 --> 00:14:44.520
It's like so predictable,
it's like so simple, almost.

00:14:44.520 --> 00:14:45.410
What is it?

00:14:45.410 --> 00:14:48.990
Why do we love to shine
a spotlight on women

00:14:48.990 --> 00:14:52.020
and analyze and talk
about what they look like

00:14:52.020 --> 00:14:53.350
and how they behave?

00:14:53.350 --> 00:14:55.386
- Now, that's almost
malfunctioning, isn't it?

00:14:55.386 --> 00:14:59.188
(audience laughing)
(laughing)

00:14:59.188 --> 00:15:00.021
Oh, wow.

00:15:02.410 --> 00:15:05.251
Man, you, very nice.
(audience applauding)

00:15:05.251 --> 00:15:06.735
- Thank you.

00:15:06.735 --> 00:15:08.570
- By the way, you smell wonderful.

00:15:08.570 --> 00:15:09.403
- We just do, like,

00:15:09.403 --> 00:15:12.160
there's just something
inside of us that does that.

00:15:12.160 --> 00:15:15.060
If you're resistant to this,
I want to hear from you.

00:15:15.060 --> 00:15:16.790
What's the counterpoint to this?

00:15:16.790 --> 00:15:18.290
I'm seeing all this stuff.

00:15:18.290 --> 00:15:19.430
I'm not just coming with this

00:15:19.430 --> 00:15:21.400
'cause this is cool and culturally trendy.

00:15:21.400 --> 00:15:23.530
I am seeing this in the data.

00:15:23.530 --> 00:15:26.320
I've seen this over years and years

00:15:26.320 --> 00:15:28.950
of observing mass human behavior

00:15:28.950 --> 00:15:31.380
with tens of thousands of data points.

00:15:31.380 --> 00:15:34.001
This is my conclusion, language matters

00:15:34.001 --> 00:15:36.510
and for some reason in our society

00:15:36.510 --> 00:15:41.510
our language around women is
very predictably scrutinizing

00:15:42.050 --> 00:15:45.260
and focused on behavior
and voice and appearance.

00:15:45.260 --> 00:15:46.990
That, to me is a fact

00:15:46.990 --> 00:15:49.410
that I'm willing to just say very clearly.

00:15:49.410 --> 00:15:52.700
And the Janet Jackson fiasco isn't about

00:15:52.700 --> 00:15:54.880
whether or not it was a
conspiracy or publicity stunt.

00:15:54.880 --> 00:15:56.210
You can have your opinions on that.

00:15:56.210 --> 00:15:58.930
But what's clear to me
is that in this instance

00:15:58.930 --> 00:16:00.650
and in many other instances,

00:16:00.650 --> 00:16:03.200
it's a horribly predictable outcome.

00:16:03.200 --> 00:16:07.010
Same old story, and frankly, I
find it slightly embarrassing

00:16:07.010 --> 00:16:11.070
and unfortunate that it takes
me looking through the data

00:16:11.070 --> 00:16:13.490
